  "worked_solution": "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the body: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=40\\cdot 9.8=392\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Hence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=392-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying Newton's second law, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\ 392-k{v}^{2} & =40a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $\\frac{49}{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}392-k\\cdot {(2\\sqrt{5})}^{2} & =40\\cdot \\frac{49}{10} \\\\ 392-20k & =196 \\\\ -20k & =-196 \\\\ k & =\\frac{196}{20} \\\\ k & =\\frac{49}{5}\\,\\text{kg/m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=392-\\frac{49}{5}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the body reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n392-\\frac{49}{5}({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,\u27f9\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{40}=2\\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the body is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
